                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
                  %h2#universal_constructions Universal constructions
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-limit"} 2-limit
                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-adjunction
                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-Kan+extension"} (∞,1)-Kan extension
                      %ul
                        %li
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+in+a+quasi-category"} (∞,1)-limit
                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-Grothendieck+construction"} (∞,1)-Grothendieck construction
                  %h2#extra_properties_and_structure Extra properties and structure
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+cube"} cosmic cube
                      %ul
                        %li
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-tuply+monoidal+n-category"} k-tuply monoidal n-category
                        %li
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+%E2%88%9E-category"}> strict ∞-category
                          ,
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} strict ∞-groupoid
                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
                  %h2#1categorical_presentations 1-categorical presentations
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+category"} homotopical category
                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category theory
                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#basic_concepts"} Basic concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#basic_constructions"} Basic constructions
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#higher_presheaves"} Higher presheaves
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#higher_universal_constructions"} Higher universal constructions
              %li
                %a{:href => "#basic_theorems"} Basic theorems
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea_of_relation_to_other_perspectives_on_higher_structures"} Idea of relation to other perspectives on higher structures
              %li
                %a{:href => "#applications"} Applications
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#extended_cobordisms"} Extended cobordisms
              %li
                %a{:href => "#models"} Models
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#via_1categorical_presentations"} Via 1-categorical presentations
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Higher category theory
            is the generalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            to a context where there are not only
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-morphism"} k-morphism
            = succeed "-morphisms," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k-1)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{N}
          %p
            Higher category theory studies the generalization of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} homotopy hypothesis
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+space"} directed space
            %em combinatorial or algebraic models
            \. It is to the theory of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            is to the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            (and hence of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
          %p
            These
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+definition+of+higher+category"} combinatorial
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+definition+of+higher+category"} algebraic
            models are known as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-category"} n-categories
            or, when
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \to \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-category"} ∞-categories
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%89-categories"} ω-categories
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2Cr%29-category"} (n,r)-categories
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the natural number
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                denotes the maximal dimension of
                %em non-trivial
                cells in the model,
            %li
              %p
                while the natural number
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
                denotes the maximal dimension of the
                %em directed
                cells.
          %p
            So an ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            is an
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C0%29-category"} (∞,0)-category
          %p
            In contrast to that, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+definition+of+higher+category"} combinatorial
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+definition+of+higher+category"} algebraic
            model for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+space"} directed space
            in which the 1-dimensional paths may not all be reversible is an
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
          %p
            Often it is convenient in practice to consider the case where the possible dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            of non-trivial cells is finite. This is familiar from how a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            that happens to have vanishing
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group"} homotopy group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            – a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-type"} homotopy n-type
            – is modeled by an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-groupoid"} n-groupoid
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-category"} n-category
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2Cn%29-category"} (n,n)-category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-category," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n,n+1)
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-poset"} poset
            = succeed "-cells" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-cells" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            = succeed "-cells" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
          %p
            For low
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            very explicit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+definition+of+higher+category"} algebraic models
            for
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            increases: the series starts with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tricategory"} tricategory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tetracategory"} tetracategory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-strict+infinity-category"} semi-strictifications
            of tricategories called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gray-category"} Gray-categories
          %p
            Indeed, there are many
            %em strictified
            models for higher categories: combinatorial or algebraic models that sacrifice full generality for a better concrete control. Notably there is a useful combinatorial/algebraic model for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+%E2%88%9E-category"} strict ∞-categories
            which, while falling short, already goes a long way towards describing general higher categorical structures. In fact, by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simpson%27s+conjecture"} Simpson's conjecture
            every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-category"} ∞-category
            is equivalent to one that looks like a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+%E2%88%9E-category"} strict ∞-category
            except for possibly having weak unit laws.
          %p
            The challenge of describing fully general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-category"} ∞-categories
            is to achieve a combinatorial or algebraic control of all the higher composition rules of higher cells. One can distinguish roughly two orthogonal approaches to dealing with the problem:
          %p
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+definition+of+higher+category"} algebraic definition of higher category
            an algebraic machinery is set up that allows to concretely handle the explicit
            %em choices
            of composites of cells. Such machinery usually involves
            = succeed "ic" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"} operad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Batanin+%E2%88%9E-category"} Batanin ∞-category
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Trimble+n-category"} Trimble ∞-category
          %p
            On the other hand, in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+definition+of+higher+category"} geometric definition of higher category
            a combinatorial machinery is set up that allows to guarantee
            %em existence
            of composites of cells. In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+for+weak+omega-categories"} simplicial models for weak ∞-categories
            higher categories are characterized as
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} property
            that certain composites exist. The issue here is to characterize these existence laws correctly.
          %p
            The basic example for such “existence laws” is the
            %em Kan-filler condition
            that characterizes simplicial sets that are
            = succeed "es," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C0%29-category"} (∞,0)-categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-categories
          %p
            The right further relaxation of the (weak) Kan filler condition is more involved. An approach to capture this has been given by
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dominic+Verity"} Dominic Verity
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complicial+set"} complicial set
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+complicial+set"} weak complicial set
          %p
            One expects that every algebraic definition of higher categories admits a construction called a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            that maps it to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            that would constitute the corresponding geometric model.
          %p
            Another approach to handle the geometric definition of higher categories is a recursive one that uses
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+Segal+space"} complete Segal space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-category
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-fold+complete+Segal+space"} n-fold complete Segal space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} (∞,n)-categories
            for finite
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Finally, a large supply of further models exists for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-categories
            in terms of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+category"} Simplicially enriched model categories
            are a highly-developed toolkit for handling
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} presentable (∞,1)-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretriangulated+dg-category"} Pretriangulated dg-enriched categories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-infinity+category"} A-∞ categories
            are a comparably highly developed toolkit for handling
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-categories
          %h2#basic_concepts Basic concepts
          %p
            The basic concept on which higher category theory is built is the notion of
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-morphism"} k-morphism
            for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{N}
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+law"}> coherence law
              s
            are satisfied.
          %p This is what it’s all about.
          %h2#basic_constructions Basic constructions
          %h3#higher_presheaves Higher presheaves
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+topos+theory"} higher topos theory
          %h3#higher_universal_constructions Higher universal constructions
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-limit"} 2-limit
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-adjunction
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-Kan+extension"} (∞,1)-Kan extension
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+in+a+quasi-category"} (∞,1)-limit
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-Grothendieck+construction"} (∞,1)-Grothendieck construction
          %h2#basic_theorems Basic theorems
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"}> homotopy hypothesis
                \-theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping+hypothesis"}> delooping hypothesis
                \-theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/periodic+table"} periodic table
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilization+hypothesis"}> stabilization hypothesis
                \-theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/michaelshulman/show/exactness+hypothesis"} exactness hypothesis
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle+of+higher+category+theory"} holographic principle
          %h2#idea_of_relation_to_other_perspectives_on_higher_structures Idea of relation to other perspectives on higher structures
          %p
            As outlined in the introduction to this article, one motivation for higher category theory comes from the relation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              \-groupoids
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            via the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} homotopy hypothesis
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %em same
            kind of higher structures. Concretely, the higher structures in this example may be called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy types
          %p
            Similarly, generalizing the homotopy hypothesis, higher categories and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+space"} higher directed spaces
            should model the same kind of higher structures:
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+homotopy+type"} directed homotopy types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            in its definition—as such, we can call directed spaces a ‘continuum’ (or ‘topological’) model of directed homotopy types. In contrast, higher categories usually need not rely on the continuum but are defined in combinatorial terms (as ‘sets of higher morphisms’ etc.)—as such, we may call them ‘combinatorial’ models of higher structures.
          %p
            One can argue that there is also a third pole of models of higher structures, namely ‘geometric’ models (here, ‘geometric’ refers to definitions in terms of
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stratified+space"} stratified manifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+definition+of+higher+categories"} geometric composition paradigm
            in combinatorial structures’). The argument is described, for instance, on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2023/03/an_invitation_to_geometric_hig.html"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                \-Category Café
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+n-category"} geometric higher categories
          %h2#applications Applications
          %p See
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/applications+of+%28higher%29+category+theory"}> applications of (higher) category theory
              \.
          %h3#extended_cobordisms Extended cobordisms
          %p
            One major application of higher category theory and one of the driving forces in developing it has been
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} extended functorial quantum field theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category+of+cobordisms"} (∞,n)-category of cobordisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord_n
            as the free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} (∞,n)-category
            with duals on a single generator.
